('128.7.b.4',2,'Talking through ideas with a partner before writing uses:','["no skills", "only spelling", "speaking and listening to support writing", "only reading"]'::jsonb,2,'Oral language supports writing.','original'),
('128.7.b.5',2,'A student interviews a classmate, takes notes and writes a profile. Which skills were integrated?','["only drawing", "only counting", "none", "listening, speaking and writing"]'::jsonb,3,'Integrated tasks use several skills.','original'),
('128.7.b.5',2,'Reading a text aloud with expression practices:','["reading and speaking together", "drawing only", "writing only", "counting"]'::jsonb,0,'Oral reading combines skills.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.A',3,'Before reading a primary source, a student might set the purpose to:','["find the longest word", "learn how people experienced an event", "count the images", "skip the introduction"]'::jsonb,1,'Purpose guides reading.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.A',3,'A reader''s purpose can change when:','["the page turns", "the title is underlined", "the reader''s goal changes", "the font changes"]'::jsonb,2,'Purposes are tied to goals.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.B',3,'A question that deepens understanding during reading is:','["Where is the cover?", "Is the page number odd?", "What time is it?", "Why did the author include this detail?"]'::jsonb,3,'Analytical questions deepen thinking.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.B',3,'Asking questions before reading helps a reader:','["activate prior knowledge", "skip the text", "avoid thinking", "forget the topic"]'::jsonb,0,'Pre-reading questions prepare the mind.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.C',3,'A reader predicts that a mystery''s detective will find the culprit. The prediction is based on:','["the page count", "genre characteristics", "the author''s birthday", "the cover weight"]'::jsonb,1,'Genre patterns guide predictions.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.C',3,'If a prediction turns out to be wrong, the reader should:','["ignore the text", "stop reading", "revise it using new evidence", "restart the book"]'::jsonb,2,'Predictions are corrected or confirmed with evidence.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.D',3,'Visualizing a scene in a poem helps readers to:','["change the rhyme", "skip the stanzas", "ignore the title", "deepen understanding of the images"]'::jsonb,3,'Mental images deepen comprehension.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.D',3,'Which sensory words help create a mental image?','["crackling, golden, smoky", "it, they, we", "the, a, an", "is, was, be"]'::jsonb,0,'Sensory words create images.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.E',3,'A student reads about immigration and thinks about a neighbor''s family history. This is a:','["table of contents", "personal connection", "glossary", "summary"]'::jsonb,1,'Connections relate texts to experience.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.E',3,'Connecting a novel to a news event is a connection to:','["the page number", "the font", "society", "the glossary"]'::jsonb,2,'Text-to-world connections link to society.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.F',3,'From the sentence ''Jo checked her backpack twice and sighed'', a reader can infer Jo is:','["sleeping", "laughing", "celebrating", "worried she forgot something"]'::jsonb,3,'Actions give clues about feelings.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.F',3,'Inferences are strongest when they are supported by:','["multiple pieces of evidence", "one guess", "the cover", "a feeling"]'::jsonb,0,'More evidence makes inferences stronger.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.G',3,'To decide which details are key, a reader asks:','["Which are in color?", "Which details support the main idea?", "Which are longest?", "Which are first?"]'::jsonb,1,'Key details support the main idea.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.G',3,'A key idea differs from a minor detail because it:','["appears only once", "is always first", "is central to the meaning of the text", "is always a number"]'::jsonb,2,'Key ideas carry the meaning.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.H',3,'Which activity shows synthesis?','["Copying a graph", "Skipping both", "Copying an article", "Combining facts from a graph and an article to form a new conclusion"]'::jsonb,3,'Synthesis merges sources.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.H',3,'Synthesis results in:','["a new understanding", "a copy of the text", "a title", "a list of page numbers"]'::jsonb,0,'Synthesis builds new meaning.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.I',3,'A reader realizes she cannot explain what she just read. A good adjustment is to:','["keep reading faster", "pause, reread and summarize", "close the book", "skip the section"]'::jsonb,1,'Fix-up strategies restore meaning.','original'),
('128.7.b.6.I',3,'Monitoring comprehension includes:','["counting pages", "guessing endings", "checking whether the text makes sense as you read", "copying sentences"]'::jsonb,2,'Monitoring is checking understanding.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.A',2,'A self-selected text is one that you:','["never read", "cannot understand", "must read for a test", "choose to read yourself"]'::jsonb,3,'Self-selected texts are chosen by the reader.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.A',3,'A personal connection response might begin:','["This reminds me of a time when I", "Page twelve says", "The publisher is", "The author''s address is"]'::jsonb,0,'Personal connections link to experience.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.B',3,'A compare-and-contrast response to two poems should discuss:','["the poets'' ages only", "similarities and differences in theme, style or imagery", "page numbers", "word counts only"]'::jsonb,1,'Comparisons examine meaning and craft.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.B',3,'Which response demonstrates understanding?','["I liked it.", "The story is a story.", "The story shows how courage can grow from fear, as when Ana speaks at the assembly.", "It was fine."]'::jsonb,2,'A strong response explains with a detail.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.C',3,'Text evidence can be shown by:','["pointing to the cover", "guessing", "skipping details", "quoting or paraphrasing a detail and explaining it"]'::jsonb,3,'Evidence is a detail plus explanation.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.C',3,'Which piece of text evidence best supports ''The wolf is cunning''?','["The wolf disguised himself as a sheep to trick the farmer.", "The wolf lives in the forest.", "The farmer has sheep.", "The wolf is gray."]'::jsonb,0,'Actions show character traits.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.D',3,'A summary of an article should:','["add your opinions only", "include main ideas in logical order", "start at the end", "include every detail"]'::jsonb,1,'Summaries are concise and ordered.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.D',3,'To paraphrase a sentence you:','["copy it", "delete it", "restate it in different words, keeping the meaning", "reverse its meaning"]'::jsonb,2,'Paraphrase keeps meaning.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.E',3,'Notetaking while reading helps because it:','["hides the text", "replaces reading", "always slows thinking", "records key ideas for later"]'::jsonb,3,'Notes capture key ideas.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.E',3,'Which is an example of interacting with a source?','["creating a sketchnote of the main ideas", "tearing it", "hiding it", "closing the book"]'::jsonb,0,'Sketchnotes are a meaningful interaction.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.F',3,'Using precise vocabulary learned in class in a response shows:','["copying", "deeper understanding", "carelessness", "avoidance"]'::jsonb,1,'Applying vocabulary shows learning.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.F',3,'A student writes: ''The explorer''s perseverance impressed me.'' The student is:','["skipping the reading", "drawing", "applying a newly learned word", "avoiding vocabulary"]'::jsonb,2,'Using new words in context shows learning.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.G',3,'In a text discussion, an important idea can be supported by:','["staying silent", "changing the subject", "guessing", "citing a specific passage"]'::jsonb,3,'Discussion draws on the text.','original'),
('128.7.b.7.G',3,'Discussing specific ideas helps a group:','["build shared understanding", "forget the meaning", "avoid the text", "end the discussion"]'::jsonb,0,'Discussion deepens meaning.','original'),
('128.7.b.8.A',3,'A novel about a girl who learns about courage and loyalty has:','["no themes", "multiple themes", "only one word", "only one setting"]'::jsonb,1,'Texts can have several themes.','original'),
('128.7.b.8.A',3,'To infer multiple themes, a reader should:','["pick one word", "guess", "find evidence for each idea in the text", "ignore events"]'::jsonb,2,'Themes need text evidence.','original'),
('128.7.b.8.B',3,'Conflict between a character and a storm is called:','["character versus society", "character versus self", "character versus time", "character versus nature"]'::jsonb,3,'The storm is a force of nature.','original'),
('128.7.b.8.B',3,'A conflict inside a character''s mind is:','["internal conflict", "the theme", "external conflict", "the setting"]'::jsonb,0,'Internal conflict happens in the character.','original'),
('128.7.b.8.C',3,'The rising action of a plot:','["ends the story", "builds tension through events and complications", "solves the problem", "introduces the title"]'::jsonb,1,'Rising action leads to the climax.','original'),
('128.7.b.8.C',3,'The resolution of a story is when:','["the title appears", "the setting is described", "the main conflict is settled", "the conflict begins"]'::jsonb,2,'The resolution ends the conflict.','original'),
('128.7.b.8.D',3,'A story set during the Great Depression shapes the plot by:','["making time irrelevant", "changing the author", "removing all problems", "creating problems tied to poverty and hardship"]'::jsonb,3,'Historical settings influence events.','original'),
('128.7.b.8.D',3,'A cultural setting influences a plot when characters:','["make choices based on their community''s customs", "speak no words", "have no relationships", "ignore all customs"]'::jsonb,0,'Culture shapes choices.','original'),
('128.7.b.9.A',3,'A legend is a story that is usually:','["a news report", "based on a historical person or event with exaggeration", "a recipe", "a dictionary entry"]'::jsonb,1,'Legends blend history and exaggeration.','original'),
('128.7.b.9.A',3,'A moral at the end of a fable is:','["the setting", "the title", "the lesson it teaches", "the author''s name"]'::jsonb,2,'Fables teach morals.','original'),
('128.7.b.9.B',3,'Alliteration is:','["exaggeration", "repetition of ending vowels", "comparison using like", "repetition of beginning consonant sounds"]'::jsonb,3,'Alliteration is a sound device.','original'),
('128.7.b.9.B',3,'In a poem, the voice that tells the poem, who may not be the poet, is the:','["speaker", "illustrator", "editor", "publisher"]'::jsonb,0,'The speaker is distinct from the poet.','original'),
('128.7.b.9.C',3,'In a script, parentheses and italics often show:','["spelling errors", "stage directions", "page numbers", "the author''s address"]'::jsonb,1,'Stage directions guide performance.','original'),
